RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk

December 20, 2017

Due to the Christmas holiday, the Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne instead met in
regular session on this date beginning at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were:  MAYOR
– Marian J. Orr; COUNCIL MEMBERS – Rocky Case, Bryan Cook, Richard Johnson, Pete
Laybourn, Scott Roybal, Dr. Mark Rinne, Dicky Shanor and Jeff White.  Absent:  Mike Luna.
Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk, and Sylvia Hackl, City Attorney. The pledge of
allegiance was recited.

Election of City Council President for 2018.  Mayor Orr announced that pursuant to Title
2 of Cheyenne City Code the Mayor conducts the election for the office of City Council President
but does not cast a vote.  Mr. Shanor moved to nominate and elect Dr. Rinne as City Council
President for 2018, seconded by Mr. Case.  Dr. Rinne accepted the nomination.  There being no
further nominations, Mr. Shanor moved that nominations cease, seconded by Mr. Case.  Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of City Council present.  Motion to nominate and elect Dr.
Rinne as City Council President for 2018 carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of City Council
present with the exception of Mr. Johnson voting “no”.

Election of City Council Vice-President for 2018.  Mayor Orr announced that pursuant to
Title 2 of Cheyenne City Code the Mayor conducts the election for the office of City Council Vice-
President but does not cast a vote.  Mr. Cook moved to nominate and elect Mr. Case as City Council
Vice-President for 2018, seconded by Dr. Rinne.  Mr. Case accepted the nomination.  There being
no further nominations, Mr. Shanor moved that nominations cease, seconded by Mr. Cook.  Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of City Council present.  Motion to nominate and elect Mr.
Case as City Council Vice-President for 2018 carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the City
Council present with the exception of Mr. Johnson voting “no”.

Consent Agenda.  (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to be
routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion. There will be no separate
discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and support by two
other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be considered in its
normal sequence on the agenda.)    Mr. Shanor stated he would be abstaining from voting (conflict
of interest involving Consent Agenda Item #17) and vacated Council Chambers.  Upon request by
Mr. Laybourn, and pursuant to support received by Mr. Case and Mr. Cook, Agenda Items #14
and 15 were removed from the Consent Agenda.  Remaining Consent Agenda items were
approved.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present in Council Chambers.
Following announcement of the vote, Mr. Shanor returned to Council Chambers.

[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on November 27, 2017.  Motion
(per Consent Agenda) carried.

Mr. Roybal declared a conflict of interest (business interest in voucher payable to Cowboy
Dodge, Inc.) and vacated Council Chambers.  Mr. Shanor moved to approve payment of vouchers
as presented, seconded by Mr. Case.  Mr. Laybourn moved to divide the question to allow for a
separate vote on the voucher payable to Noel Griffith Jr. & Associates, seconded by Mr. Case.
```

### Page 2 of 5

```text
Motion to divide the question carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present
in Council Chambers.  In response to a procedural question, Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk, explained
when an item is divided it is considered and voted upon separately and that a separate motion to
approve is not needed as the motion to divide splits the main motion into one or more parts.  Mr.
Laybourn moved to postpone action on the voucher payable to Noel Griffith Jr. & Associates to
January 8, 2018, seconded by Mr. Case.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the
governing body present in Council Chambers.  Motion to approve remaining vouchers carried.
Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present in Council Chambers with the exception
of Mr. Johnson voting “no”.  Following announcement of the vote, Mr. Roybal returned to Council
Chambers.  Under other business later in the meeting, clarification was given to staff pertaining to
information to be provided prior to the next Council meeting regarding the postponed voucher.

ORDINANCE – 3rd READING – Annexing to the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming, a portion
of Section 9, all of the W½ Section 14, all of Section 15, a portion of Section 16, and all of Section
21; Township 13 North, Range 66 West of the 6th P.M., Laramie County, Wyoming (located to
the southeast of the intersection of E. College Drive and Avenue C) (postponed from 12/11/2017).
Reporting for Public Services Committee, Mr. Roybal moved to approve on third and final reading,
seconded by Mr. Johnson.  Mr. Roybal moved to postpone to January 8, 2018 at the request of
staff, seconded by Mr. Shanor.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body present.

ORDINANCE – 3rd READING – Amending various sections of Title 10, Vehicles and
Traffic, of the code of the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming.  Mr. Roybal moved to approve on third
and final reading, seconded by Mr. White.  Upon inquiry pertaining to a previously approved
amendment changing the signed parking enforcement distance from 100 ft. to 150 ft., Sylvia
Hackl, City Attorney, expressed her opinion that specificity of the number of feet vs. a reference
to a portion of a block is more appropriate, and listing the specific footage is defensible.  Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present. (#4213)

ORDINANCE – 2nd READING – Pursuant to Sections 1.1.6 and 2.4.1 of the Cheyenne
Unified Development Code (UDC), amending Section 1.4.4.f., Industrial Use definition, of the
UDC to clarify what land uses are subject to the Moderate Industrial and the Industrial use
categories.  Mr. Roybal moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr. Case.  Comment
followed on property use for vehicle impoundment storage areas for public vs. private sectors.
Susana Montana, Planning and Development Department, advised staff will be recommending an
amendment at the next Public Services Committee meeting to define the term “impoundment
yard”.  Upon inquiry, Ms. Montana stated the conditional use would pertain to all commercial and
industrial districts, and that the new definition would be included in the current definition section
of the UDC.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present with the
exception of Dr. Rinne voting “no”.

RESOLUTION – Authorizing the Mayor and the City Clerk to sign a County Final Plat for
Clear Creek Subdivision, 1st Filing, being situated in portions of Sections 10, 11, 14, 15 and 16,
Township 13 North, Range 67 West of the Sixth Principal Meridian, Laramie County, Wyoming
(located west of I-25 between College Drive and I-80).  Mr. Roybal, seconded by Mr. Case, moved
to adopt with revised plat map and the following staff condition #1:  Include the Mayor’s and City
Clerk’s names and titles on the signature block in the dedication statement.  Upon inquiry, Seth
Lloyd, Planning and Development Department, explained the Mayor and City Clerk information
```

